B. Random OTP generation — mandatory

Implement a fully functional server-side OTP generation and verification system.

Requirements:

Generate a cryptographically secure random OTP whenever verification is requested.

Never use a fixed, predictable or hardcoded OTP.

Generate a separate OTP challenge for each verification request.

Store OTP challenges securely on the backend.

Set a configurable expiry time.

Implement resend cooldown.

Limit verification attempts.

Invalidate an OTP after successful verification.

Prevent OTP reuse.

Prevent abuse through rate limiting.

Handle expired and incorrect OTPs properly.

Provide clear verification feedback.

Website-visible OTP for development and testing

I specifically require the generated OTP to be visible on the website during development/testing so the entire login and verification workflow can be tested without depending on an external SMS provider.

Implement two clearly separated modes:

DEVELOPMENT / TEST MODE

Generate a new random OTP on the backend.

Display the generated OTP in a clearly labelled testing panel on the verification screen.

Display it only for the current test session and intended test account.

Allow the user to copy the OTP.

Verify it against the server-side challenge.

Show expiry and resend countdown.

Do not use a permanently fixed OTP.

Never expose OTPs belonging to another user.

PRODUCTION MODE

Send the generated OTP through the actual SMS provider to the user's registered mobile number.

Send email OTPs through the configured email provider.

Do not expose the production OTP in frontend responses, browser logs, public APIs or the verification screen.

Verify the OTP securely on the backend.

The application must automatically respect its configured environment mode. Never allow a production user to activate development OTP display through a frontend setting.

C. Actual SMS and email verification

Integrate an actual SMS provider such as MSG91 or Twilio.

Integrate an actual email provider such as Resend or SendGrid.

When a citizen enters their mobile number:

Validate the Indian mobile number.

Generate a random OTP.

Send the OTP to that number.

Display a confirmation that the SMS request was accepted by the provider.

Allow OTP verification.

Update phone verification status only after successful verification.

When a citizen enters their email:

Generate a separate random OTP.

Send it to the registered email.

Verify it independently.

Update email verification status only after successful verification.

Do not show "OTP sent successfully" unless the provider confirms successful acceptance.

Show useful error messages when delivery fails.

